,\mathcal{V}}+s\left({C\&D\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)},u\right).
\end{align*}
Then $\mathcal{T}$ is a~bounded nonnegative quadratic form. Hence, by Kato's First Representation Theorem, there exists some nonnegative and self-adjoint $M\in \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{b}}(\mathcal{W})$, such that
$\mathcal{T}(v)=\scprod{Mv}{v}_{\mathcal{W}}$ for all $v\in\mathcal{W}$. Then, by \cite[Chap.~V, Thm.~3.35]{Kato80}, $M$ possesses an operator square root $\sqrt{M}\in \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{b}}(\mathcal{W})$, i.e., $\sqrt{M}$ is nonnegative, self-adjoint, and it fulfills $\sqrt{M}^2=M$. Now setting $\mathcal{Z}:=\overline{\operatorname{im} \sqrt{M}}$ (endowed with the inner product inherited from $\mathcal{W}$) and $K\&L:=\sqrt{M}$, we have that $K\&L\in \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{b}}(\mathcal{W},\mathcal{Z})$ has dense range and
\begin{align*}
\forall\,\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in\mathcal{W}:\quad
&\phantom{=}2\operatorname{Re}\scprod*{\widetilde{P}x}{A\&B \left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_{\mathcal{V} 
