  ^{\the\striche} 
,\mathcal{V}}\geq -s\bigl(y_n(t),u_n(t)\bigr).
\]
Now an integration over $[0,T-1/n]$ gives
\begin{equation}
\forall\,n\in{\mathbb{N}} \quad \mathcal{S}(x_n(T-1/n))-\mathcal{S}(x_n(0))+ \int_0^{T-1/n}s\bigl(y_n(t),u_n(t)\bigr)\mathop{\frac{\text{\normalfont d}}{\text{\normalfont d}t}}\geq0.\label{eq:apprdiss}
\end{equation}
It follows from the mean value theorem of integration together with uniform continuity of $x(\cdot):[0,T]\to\mathcal{V}$ that $(x_n(\cdot))$ converges to $x(\cdot)$ in $\mathcal{V}$. Further, by \cite[Thm.~4.15]{Alt16}, 
we have
\[\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_0^{T-1/n}s\bigl(y_n(t),u_n(t)\bigr)\mathop{\frac{\text{\normalfont d}}{\text{\normalfont d}t}}=\int_0^Ts\bigl(y(t),u(t)\bigr)\mathop{\frac{\text{\normalfont d}}{\text{\normalfont d}t}}.\]
Now taking the limit $n\to\infty$ in \eqref{eq:apprdiss}, we obtain that the dissipation inequality holds.

\label{rem:dissineq}
Assume the framework of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. 
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph{*})]
\item\label{rem:dissineq1} Consider the space
\[\mathcal{W}_P^0:=\setdef{\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in \operatorname{dom}(A\&B)}{x\in \operatorname{dom}(P)},\]
which is a~Hilbert space endowed with the norm
\[\norm*{\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_{\mathcal{W}_P^0}^2=\norm*{\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_{\mathcal{V}}^2+\|Px\|^2_\mathcal{X}.\]
Under the additional assumptions that 
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{W}^1_P:=\setdef{\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in \mathcal{V}}{x\in \operatorname{dom}(P)}\label{eq:domPdense} 
\end{equation} is dense
in both $\mathcal{W}$ and $\mathcal{W}_P^0$, the statements in Theorem~\ref{thm:main} are equivalent to
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman{*}), start=6]
\item\label{item:dissineq6} $\mathcal{W}\subset\operatorname{dom}(\mathcal{S})$ and 
  \begin{multline*}
\forall\,    \left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in\operatorname{dom}(A\&B)\text{ with }x\in\operatorname{dom}(P):\\
2\operatorname{Re}\scprod*{Px}{A\&B \left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_{\mathcal{X}}+s\left({C\&D\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)},u\right)\geq 0.
  \end{multline*}
\end{enumerate}
To verify this, we first state that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KYPdomP}
\begin{aligned}
\forall\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in\mathcal{W}^1_P:\quad &\phantom{=}\;\;2\operatorname{Re}\scprod*{Px}{A\&B \left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_{\mathcal{X}}\quad\;\;\;+s\left({C\&D\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)},u\right)\\&=2\operatorname{Re}\scprod*{\widetilde{P}x}{A\&B \left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_{\mathcal{V} 
  \setcounter{i}{1} 
  \striche={\ast} 
  \loop 
  \ifnum\value{i}<1 
  \striche=\expandafter{\the\expandafter\striche\ast} 
  \stepcounter{i} 
  \repeat 
  ^{\the\striche} 
,\mathcal{V}}+s\left({C\&D\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)},u\right).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Consequently, by using density of $\mathcal{W}^1_P$ in $\mathcal{W}$, we see that
\ref{item:dissineq6} implies \ref{item:dissineq2}. On the other hand, if \ref{item:dissineq3} holds, then \eqref{eq:KYPdomP} implies that
  \[
\forall\,    \left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in\mathcal{W}^1_P:\qquad
2\operatorname{Re}\scprod*{Px}{A\&B \left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_{\mathcal{X}}+s\left({C\&D\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)},u\right)\geq 0.
  \]
Then density of $\mathcal{W}^1_P$ in $\mathcal{W}^0_P$ yields, together with a~continuity argument, that  \ref{item:dissineq6} is valid.\\
The author of this article is not certain whether the above density assumptions are always satisfied. In particular, no counterexample is known to him.
\item\label{rem:dissineq2} If \eqref{eq:ODEnode} is dissipative with respect to the supply rate functional $s$ and storage function $\mathcal{S}$,
then Theorem~\ref{thm:main} yields that there exists a~Hilbert space $\mathcal{Z}$ and some
$K\&L\in \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{b}}(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{Z})$ with dense range, such that \eqref{eq:kyp_inf_lure} holds for all $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x\\u\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in\mathcal{V}$.
Now using the argumentation as in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, we obtain that any classical trajectory $(x(\cdot),u(\cdot),y(\cdot))$ of \eqref{eq:ODEnode} on $[0,T]$ fulfills
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}(x(T))-\mathcal{S}(x(0))+ \int_0^Ts\bigl(y(t),u(t)\bigr)\mathop{\frac{\text{\normalfont d}}{\text{\normalfont d}t}}=\int_0^T\norm*{K\&L\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x(t)\\u(t)\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_\mathcal{Z}^2\mathop{\frac{\text{\normalfont d}}{\text{\normalfont d}t}}.\label{eq:dissrate}\end{equation}
The term $\norm*{K\&L\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x(t)\\u(t)\end{smallmatrix}\right)}_\mathcal{Z}^2$ refers to what is called {\em dissipation rate} in \cite{Will71a}.\\
Note that \eqref{eq:dissrate} implies that, for any classical trajectory $(x(\cdot),u(\cdot),y(\cdot))$ of \eqref{eq:ODEnode} on $[0,T]$, it holds that \begin{equation}
K\&L\left(\begin{smallmatrix}x(\cdot)\\u(\cdot)\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in\mathrm{L}^{2}([0,T];\mathcal{Z}).\label{eq:dissL2}
\end{equation}
\item\label{rem:dissineq3} For $k\in{\mathbb{N}}$, define
